Z||000017||chester||08-01-2001||02:54 PM||||One thing I noticed is that no one mentioned tire adhesion or size.  I used to have a set of BFGoodrich Euro T/A's on my GT and I could roast them all day long. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> These were also 15" tires.  Now I have some Dunlop SP9000 rubber in a 17" with a built 2.8 and if I get too froggy (pop the clutch) I will lift the front wheels off the ground and hook up but not "burn rubber". <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||6
Z||000018||chester||08-01-2001||02:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>if you just wanna do it to impress people, rig up a couple windshield washer pumps to a bottle of soapy water, or clear mineral oil to spray on the front of your tires from an on dash button.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> POOF.......instant block long wheelspin</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hear that bleach works well also  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Capt Fiero||08-01-2001||04:45 PM||||VHT "Trackbite" is the best stuff to use as a tire burn-out prep.  But and this will probably put some of you on the floor laughing.  I was at the local street racing industrial park early this summer, (mid febuary) It was a cold (32'F-- 0'C) but very dry night. We were doing small burnouts and stuff just shooting the breeze.  Well someone got the bright idea to see what other chemicals could be used to do burn outs.  And so the fun started, everybody went to there cars, trucks, borrowd SUV's and found anything liquid, we tried Turtle Wax, and dang that stuff is slippery, but hard to get off the tires. Next was armor all vinyle protector, hmm not bad but left a nasty odour smell in the air. Windhshield washer fluid seemed to work pretty well, but froze if left on ground to long.  The best one that we found was "drumb roll please" Safeway Brand Generic Cola, LOL.  You pour a little on the pavment and drive forward into it. Then do your normal clutch dump, if you can keep the car going straight as it burns off the tires, the sugar becomes very sticky and actully makes the tires stick so well that you can hear it on the tires as you back up, same sound that you get when you have dried pop on the bottom of a rubber soled shoe.  The only problew was if you had really wide tires, without mud flaps or skirts it would get the soda on your car and if you have good paint it is not recomended as the stuff is like tar to get off of paint.  But it was just a bunch of us acting like 2 year olds having a blast. Adults always seem to have the most fun when they become children in large groups.  P.S. My burnout pic was done on DRY pavment. <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415">  <p>[This message has been edited by Capt Fiero (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||PBJ||08-01-2001||05:56 PM||||I agree with chester on the BFG's I can lite mine up any time from a stop, with a stock v6 4sp "one wheel peeeel" till it's shift time. and my wifes v6 auto can chirp em with just one person in the car straight ahead, and around the corners any time.  <P>so you better get some BFG's and burn'em.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/petesigsm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||maryjane||08-01-2001||06:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B> [TROLL]And a completely useless engine.[/TROLL]   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not completely useless. It keeps me from walkin and I'm really glad it has enough umph to turn the a/c compressor. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 2.5<BR>not slow not fast-just halfast||||reg||1
